  "result": "I added `click.KeyValue`, and `@click.option('--set', type=click.KeyValue())` now parses `key=value` into a `(key, value)` tuple. I ran it through `CliRunner` with five inputs, but I did not run the project's test suite or add test cases.\n\n| Input | Result |\n|---|---|\n| `a=b` | `('a', 'b')` |\n| `a=b=c` | `('a', 'b=c')` |\n| `a=` | `('a', '')` |\n| `novalue` | exit code 2, `'novalue' is not a valid key=value pair.` |\n| `=x` | exit code 2, `'=x' is not a valid key=value pair.` |\n\n- **Splitting:** the string is split on the first `=` only, so the value can contain more `=` characters. An empty value is allowed; only a missing `=` or an empty key fails.\n- **Where it lives:** the class is in `src/click/types.py`, exported from `src/click/__init__.py`, and listed in the Types section of `docs/api.md`.\n- **Version note:** the docstring says `versionadded:: 8.4`. I guessed that number, so please check it against your next release.",
